# member_const_visibility_leak_rejected.tkr — the PARSE-STAGE compile-fail fold host.
#
# HONEST NAMING NOTE, first, because the directory name no longer describes the contents: the
# member-const visibility regression this project was named for MOVED to
# examples/regressions/const_ns_qualified_visibility_rejected (`src/leakbase.tks` +
# `src/leaksub/sub.tks`), where it is still asserted. It had to move: a parse error stops the
# compiler BEFORE the checker runs, so a checker-stage assertion can never fire in a project whose
# job is to fail in the parser. The owner's mandate fixes the regressive projects at nine, so the
# two compile-fail projects are the only two failing builds available and each had to take one
# PHASE. Renaming the directory was not done here because the name is referenced from the release
# manifest and the CI, which this wagon does not touch.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOW: twenty-four constructs the PARSER must reject, one per namespace under `src/`,
# every one of them a `Given source inline` snippet in regressor.tkr until this wagon — each paying
# for its own failing build. They now share ONE: a compile-fail scenario with no `Given source`
# is checked against the project's own failing build, cached across the whole file, and the parser
# reports every file's diagnostic rather than stopping at the first. Measured: 24 scenarios, 1 build.

Feature: parser rejections, folded into this project's single failing build

